course: dictionary
lesson: 7676
title: Propulsion: the force that pushes things along
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: Propulsion is the force that pushes something forward. When you meet a new word, look at the words around it, because they give you clues. «A squid moves fast by jet propulsion: it squirts water out through a funnel called a siphon and shoots the other way.» The squid pushes water one way, and it goes the other way, so propulsion must be the push that moves it.
explain: Propulsion is a noun, and it is uncountable, so you say some propulsion, not a propulsion. You will meet it in science in phrases like «jet propulsion», «rocket propulsion» and «means of propulsion». A means of propulsion is a way of moving something, such as a propeller on a boat.
explain: To check your meaning, swap it into the sentence. «A squid moves fast by the force that pushes it forward» still makes sense, so you have understood the word. Scientists use the same idea for cars, boats, rockets and animals.
fact: Propulsion means the force that pushes something forward.
fact: «Jet propulsion» means moving by pushing a stream of water or gas out one way, so the thing shoots the other way.
fact: A squid uses jet propulsion: it squirts water out through its siphon.
fact: A boat can use a propeller for propulsion.
fact: Propulsion is uncountable, so write «rocket propulsion», not «a propulsion».
exercise: «A squid moves fast by jet propulsion.» What is propulsion? | *The force that pushes something forward | A kind of water pipe | A type of squid | The name of a fish's fin
exercise: «It squirts water out through a funnel called a siphon.» What does the squid squirt water out through? | A rocket | A fin | *A funnel called a siphon | Its eyes
exercise: Which of these is a means of propulsion for a boat? | A cloud in the sky | A rock on the beach | A quiet pond | *A propeller
test: Which phrase uses propulsion correctly, as used in science? | rocket pulse | *rocket propulsion | rocket pull | rocket propel
test: How does a squid use jet propulsion? | It climbs up rocks | It hides in the sand | *It squirts water out through its siphon and shoots the other way | It sleeps on the seabed
exam: Propulsion is best described as: | a word for stopping | a fish's colour | *the force that pushes something forward | a type of rocket
